.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
.TH AUDIAKI 1 "2026-08-06" "audiaki 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
audiaki, audiaki\-gui \- record a capture device to a WAV file, tune an
instrument on it, play a take back, and visualise it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B audiaki
[\fIOPTION\fR]... \fIOUTPUT.WAV\fR
.br
.B audiaki
\fB\-\-visualize\fR \fIINPUT.WAV\fR [\fB\-o\fR \fIOUTPUT.MP4\fR]
.br
.B audiaki
\fB\-\-info\fR \fIINPUT.WAV\fR
.br
.B audiaki
\fB\-\-play\fR \fIINPUT.WAV\fR [\fIMORE.WAV\fR ...] [\fB\-D\fR \fIOUTPUT\fR]
.br
.B audiaki
\fB\-\-render\fR \fIPROJECT.AKI\fR [\fB\-o\fR \fIMIX.WAV\fR]
.br
.B audiaki
\fB\-\-tune\fR [\fB\-D\fR \fIDEVICE\fR]
.br
.B audiaki
\fB\-\-probe\fR [\fB\-D\fR \fIDEVICE\fR]
.br
.B audiaki
\fB\-\-list\fR
.br
.B audiaki\-gui
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B audiaki
opens a capture device, negotiates the widest sample format available, and
streams the audio into a canonical PCM WAV file while drawing a live peak meter
on standard error.
.PP
It talks to either PipeWire or ALSA, and chooses between them without being
told: if a PipeWire daemon answers it is used, and otherwise ALSA. See
.B \-\-backend
under
.BR "Common" ", and " BACKENDS .
.PP
The WAV header is written with placeholder sizes and patched when recording
stops, including when it is stopped with
.BR SIGINT " or " SIGTERM ,
so interrupted recordings are still valid files.
.PP
With
.B \-\-visualize
it instead reads a WAV file, analyses it, rasterises each video frame and pipes
them to
.BR ffmpeg (1),
which encodes them and muxes in the original audio. Nothing is captured and no
sound card is needed in this mode.
.PP
With
.B \-\-info
it reads a WAV file and reports what is in it \- levels, clipping and noise
floor \- then exits. This too needs no sound card.
.PP
With
.B \-\-play
it reads a WAV file and plays it through an output, drawing the same meter
against the file's length. Nothing is captured and nothing is written. See
.B PLAYBACK
below.
.PP
With
.B \-\-tune
it opens the capture device and reports the pitch of whatever is being played,
as a note and how far off that note it is in cents, until interrupted. Nothing
is written. See
.B TUNER
below.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Recording
.TP
.BR \-D ", " \-\-device " " \fINAME\fR
Capture device, spelled the way the selected backend spells it: an ALSA device
such as
.B hw:CARD=Box,DEV=0
or a PipeWire node name such as
.BR alsa_input.usb\-Box.analog\-stereo .
Defaults to the value of
.B AUDIAKI_DEVICE
or, when that is unset,
.BR default .
Run
.B \-\-list
to see what the selected backend offers.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-rate " " \fIHZ\fR
Sample rate in Hz (default 44100). If the device cannot provide it, the nearest
supported rate is used and a warning is printed.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-channels " " \fIN\fR
How many channels to capture (default 2).
.TP
.BI \-\-channel " N"
Write only capture channel
.IR N ,
counting from 1, as a mono take. The device is still opened with
.B \-\-channels
channels; this decides which one reaches the file, for an interface that only
offers stereo with a single input in use. The meter and the spectrum follow the
picked channel, so the level being set is the level that is written. Monitoring
is unaffected and still plays every channel the device delivered. Asking for a
channel the device did not negotiate is an error.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-format " " \fINAME\fR
Force a capture format:
.BR s16_le ", " s24_3le ", " s24_le " or " s32_le .
By default the best format the device offers is chosen, preferring
.B s32_le
then
.BR s24_3le ", " s24_le " and " s16_le .
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-duration " " \fISPEC\fR
Stop after the given time, written as
.IR SS ", " MM:SS " or " HH:MM:SS .
Fractional seconds are accepted. Recording stops on an exact frame count.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-period " " \fIFRAMES\fR
Period size in frames (default 1024). Larger values are more robust against
overruns at the cost of latency in the meter display.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-periods " " \fIN\fR
Periods per buffer (default 4).
.TP
.BR \-\-take " " \fIPREFIX\fR
Name the take automatically: write the first
.RI \fIPREFIX\fR \-001.wav
that does not already exist, then
.BR \-002 ,
and so on. Numbers are padded to three digits and grow beyond that if a session
runs long, up to 9999.
.IP
A prefix that already carries an extension keeps it, so
.B \-\-take session.wav
also writes
.IR session\-001.wav ,
and a path works as well as a bare name. Because the chosen name is free by
construction, nothing is ever overwritten and
.B \-\-force
does not apply. Naming an output file as well is an error.
.TP
.BR \-\-dir " " \fIFOLDER\fR
Keep takes in
.IR FOLDER ,
creating it if it is not there. A take named without a folder \(em
.BR \-o " " riff.wav ", or a " \-\-take " prefix \(em is written inside it."
.IP
A name that already says where it goes is left alone: a relative name with a
slash in it, such as
.IR sub/riff.wav ,
is relative to the working directory as it always was, and an absolute path is
itself. Overrides
.B take_dir
from the configuration file; see
.BR FILES .
.TP
.B \-\-prompt
When the take is over, ask which folder to keep it in and what to call it, and
move it there; see
.B KEEPING A TAKE
below.
Without this the question is asked only when standard input and standard error
are both a terminal and
.B \-\-quiet
was not given.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-prompt
Never ask, whatever the configuration file says.
.TP
.BR \-\-preroll " " \fISECS\fR
Hold
.I SECS
of audio and wait, instead of recording at once. The device opens and the meter
runs, but nothing is written until Enter is pressed; the take then begins with
the seconds leading up to the keypress. Accepts the same forms as
.BR \-\-duration ,
up to 300 seconds.
.IP
Interrupting the wait with Ctrl+C creates no file at all. Pressing Enter is what
starts the take, so
.B \-\-duration
is measured from that moment and the finished file is the pre\-roll plus the
duration. When standard input reaches its end - a pipe, or
.I /dev/null
\- there is no key left to wait for and recording starts immediately.
.IP
The buffer is resident memory: a minute of 24\-bit stereo at 48 kHz is about
17 MiB, and a wider or faster stream is proportionally more.
.TP
.BR \-\-note " " \fITEXT\fR
Stamp the take with a note, up to 200 characters, readable again with
.BR \-\-info .
It goes in both metadata chunks: the LIST/INFO comment tag that taggers and
players read, and the bext description field.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-metadata
Write the plain 44 byte header, with nothing between the fmt and data chunks.
Takes are stamped with the software, the date and time, the capture device and
the signal chain by default; this is for a tool that wants none of it. See
.B TAKE METADATA
below.
.TP
.B \-\-spectrum
Draw live spectrum bars instead of the peak bar. The number of bars follows the
terminal width. Block drawing characters are used when the locale is UTF-8, and
an ASCII ramp otherwise.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-meter
Do not draw anything while recording. The display is suppressed automatically
when standard error is not a terminal.
.TP
.BR \-M ", " \-\-monitor
Play the capture stream through an output while it is being recorded, so the
take can be heard as it is made. Off unless asked for.
.IP
.B Use headphones.
Monitoring an open microphone through speakers is a feedback loop: a laptop's
built\-in microphone played out of the speaker beside it reaches full scale in a
fraction of a second. Capturing an output's monitor source and playing it back
through that same output is the same loop without the air in it, and headphones
do not help with that one.
.IP
Monitoring is audible while
.B \-\-preroll
is armed as well, which is when the level is usually being set. What is heard
arrives through a buffer of its own \- tens of milliseconds under ALSA, around a
tenth of a second under PipeWire \- so it is for checking a sound rather than for
playing in time against. Frames the output cannot keep up with are dropped
rather than queued, and never reach the file;
.B \-\-verbose
reports the count at the end.
.IP
An output that will not open, or one that fails part way through, costs the
monitoring and nothing else: audiaki says so and carries on recording.
.TP
.BR \-\-monitor\-device " " \fINAME\fR
The output to monitor through, rather than the default one. Implies
.BR \-\-monitor ,
unless
.B \-\-click
was given as well: the metronome plays through this same output without the
input going near it, so there the flag names where the click comes out and
leaves monitoring alone.
Under ALSA the output has to accept the capture rate directly, because audiaki
does not resample; the
.B plug
layer converts, as in
.IR plughw:CARD=Box,DEV=0 .
.TP
.BR \-\-monitor\-gain " " \fIX\fR
Scale what is monitored, from 0.0 (silent) to 2.0 (+6 dB), default 1.0. Implies
.BR \-\-monitor .
This is the monitoring level and nothing else: a quiet monitor does not make a
quiet take, and the meter does not move with it. It does not touch the click
either. See
.B \-\-gain
for the one that does reach the file.
.TP
.BR \-\-gain " " \fIX\fR
Scale the capture on the way in, from 0.0 (silence) to 16.0 (+24 dB), default
1.0. Unlike
.BR \-\-monitor\-gain ,
this one reaches the file.
.IP
For an input with no usable level control of its own \- a line input, a cheap
USB interface, a card whose capture volume ALSA does not expose. Turn the
hardware up first where there is a knob to turn: a gain stage ahead of the
converter captures more of its range, where this only scales the range already
captured.
.IP
Applied to each period as it arrives, before the meter, the spectrum, the
pre\-roll and monitoring, so all of them describe the recording rather than the
device. Samples pushed past full scale are held there rather than wrapped, and
the count of them is reported when the take ends \- a gain set too high damages
the take in a way nothing afterwards will undo, where a take that came in quiet
is merely quiet and can be brought up later without loss.
.IP
Settable once as
.I gain
in the configuration file; see
.BR FILES .
.TP
.BR \-\-click " " \fIBPM\fR
Play a metronome at
.I BPM
beats per minute, from 20 to 300, through the output monitoring uses. Opens that
output on its own, so
.B \-M
is only needed when you want to hear the input as well;
.B \-\-monitor\-device
names the output either way.
.IP
The click is heard and never written: the take is made of the samples the device
delivered, exactly as with
.BR \-\-monitor\-gain .
Use headphones — from a speaker the click is in the room, and the input will
record it after all.
.IP
With
.B \-\-preroll
the metronome starts as soon as the recorder is armed, so the wait before Enter
is a count\-in.
.IP
The grid is counted in captured frames, so it cannot drift against the
recording: at 120 BPM and 48 kHz the clicks are generated at frames 0, 24000,
48000 and so on. What you hear arrives through the output's own buffer, though,
so a take played in time with the click is late against that grid by however
long the buffer is. It keeps the tempo; it does not sample\-align the take.
.TP
.BR \-\-click\-beats " " \fIN\fR
Beats to a bar, 0 to 32, default 4. The first beat of each bar is a tone an
octave above the others.
.B 0
and
.B 1
both mean a bare pulse with no accent. Needs
.BR \-\-click ,
which is what sets the tempo and turns the metronome on.
.TP
.BR \-\-click\-gain " " \fIX\fR
How loud the click is, from 0.0 (silent) to 2.0, default 0.5, on the same scale
as
.BR \-\-monitor\-gain
and independent of it. Needs
.BR \-\-click .
.SS Visualiser
.TP
.BR \-\-visualize " " \fIFILE\fR
Render
.I FILE
to a video and exit, instead of recording.
.I FILE
may be 8, 16, 24 or 32 bit PCM, or 32 or 64 bit float WAV, with any number of
channels; the channels are averaged for analysis and the untouched original is
what ends up in the video's audio track.
.B \-\-visualise
is accepted as a spelling of the same option.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIFILE\fR
Video file to write. Defaults to the input path with its extension replaced by
.BR .mp4 .
The container is inferred from the suffix by
.BR ffmpeg (1).
This option also names the output when recording.
.TP
.BR \-\-style " " \fINAME\fR
What each frame shows:
.BR bars " (default), " scope " or " waveform .
.RS
.TP
.B bars
The spectrum at that instant, as log-spaced bars with peak markers.
.TP
.B scope
An oscilloscope trace of the last 40 ms, drawn from the sample values
themselves. Its colour follows level rather than frequency, because its
horizontal axis is time.
.TP
.B waveform
The envelope of the whole take, with a playhead sweeping across it and the part
already heard picked out. The file is read once beforehand to reduce it to one
minimum/maximum pair per column, so the cost of that pass follows the width of
the video rather than the length of the recording.
.RE
.TP
.BR \-\-size " " \fISPEC\fR
Frame size as
.IR WxH ,
or one of
.BR 480p ", " 720p ", " 1080p ", " 1440p " or " 2160p
(default 1280x720). Both dimensions must be even, because the H.264 encoder
subsamples chroma by two.
.TP
.BR \-\-fps " " \fIN\fR
Video frame rate, 1 to 240 (default 60).
.TP
.BR \-\-bars " " \fIN\fR
Number of spectrum bars, 4 to 512 (default 64). Applies to
.B \-\-style bars
only.
.SS Playback
.TP
.BR \-\-play " " \fIFILE\fR
Play
.I FILE
through an output and exit. Whatever the reader accepts is playable, not only
audiaki's own takes.
.B \-D
names the output rather than a capture device, and defaults to
.BR default ;
.B $AUDIAKI_DEVICE
is ignored here, because what it names is an input.
.B \-t
stops early, and
.BR \-\-spectrum " and " \-\-no\-meter
apply as they do when recording. No file is written, and naming one with
.B \-o
is an error.
.IP
Files named after
.I FILE
are played after it, in the order given, so a shell glob is a playlist:
.B audiaki \-\-play session\-*.wav
plays a whole session's takes one after another.
.B \-t
applies to each of them rather than to the run.
.IP
A file that cannot be read does not end the run \- the rest is still worth
hearing \- but the exit status still reports that something was wrong.
.TP
.B Playback keys
When both standard input and standard error are a terminal, playback takes
single keys as it runs. Nothing has to be pressed and nothing is required: over
a pipe, or from a script, the keys are simply never read and each file plays
from beginning to end.
.RS
.TP 12
.B space
Pause, or carry on. What the output already holds is heard first, so a pause
takes a buffer's worth of audio to fall silent.
.TP
.BR \(<- " / " \(->
Back or forward five seconds.
.TP
.BR \(ua " / " \(da
Forward or back thirty seconds.
.TP
.B home
Back to the start of the file.
.TP
.B end
On to the end of it, which finishes the file the way playing it out would.
.TP
.BR n " / " p
The next file, or the previous one. Neither waits for what is queued, so a skip
is heard at once.
.B p
on the first file starts it again.
.TP
.B q
Stop, whatever is left of the playlist.
.RE
.IP
.B Ctrl\-C
still stops everything, as it does everywhere else in audiaki. The position the
keys move around in is the position of what has been handed to the output,
which is a buffer ahead of what is coming out of it.
.SS Sessions
.TP
.BR \-\-render " " \fIFILE\fR
Mix the session in
.I FILE
down to a WAV and exit.
.B audiaki\-gui
writes these: a session is the cuts, the levels and the clip layout made over a
set of takes, and it refers to those takes rather than containing them, so it
is a few kilobytes of readable text whatever it holds.
.IP
.B \-o
names the mix and defaults to the session's own name with
.B .wav
on it.
.B \-y
is what allows an existing mix to be replaced. No device is opened, so this
runs anywhere \- over ssh, in a build, on a machine with no sound server. A
take the session refers to that is no longer where it says it is is named, and
nothing is written.
.TP
.BR \-\-bits " " \fIN\fR
Bit depth
.B \-\-render
writes:
.BR 16 ", " 24 " or " 32
(default 24). Applies to no other command, a take being written at whatever
the device gave.
.SS Tuner
.TP
.B \-\-tune
Show the pitch of what is being played and exit on
.BR SIGINT " or " SIGTERM .
No file is written, and naming one is an error. The capture options above
still apply, so
.BR \-D ", " \-r " and " \-c
choose the device to listen on.
.TP
.BR \-\-a4 " " \fIHZ\fR
The reference pitch every note is measured against, 390 to 500 (default 440).
Set it to play with an ensemble tuned somewhere other than concert pitch.
.SS Common
.TP
.BR \-\-backend " " \fINAME\fR
Audio system to talk to:
.BR auto ", " pipewire " or " alsa
(default
.BR auto ,
or the value of
.BR AUDIAKI_BACKEND ).
.B auto
uses PipeWire when its daemon answers and ALSA otherwise. Naming a backend that
is unavailable is an error rather than a fallback. See
.BR BACKENDS .
.TP
.BR \-\-info " " \fIFILE\fR
Measure
.I FILE
and exit. Reports its format and duration, the peak and RMS level in dBFS, an
estimated noise floor, how many samples reached full scale, and the peak, RMS
and DC offset of each channel separately, followed by whatever the file says
about itself. See
.B TAKE REPORT
below.
.IP
Further files may follow the first, and then each is reported as a single row
instead \- which is the shape the question takes at the end of a session:
.IP
.nf
audiaki \-\-info session\-*.wav
.fi
.IP
A file that cannot be read is reported on standard error and stepped over, so
one unreadable take does not hide the state of the rest. The exit status is
still non\-zero.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Write the output of
.BR \-\-list ", " \-\-probe " or " \-\-info
as JSON on standard output instead of aligned text. Diagnostics stay on
standard error, so the stream is safe to pipe into a parser. Using it with any
other command is an error.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-force
Overwrite the output file if it already exists. Without this flag an existing
file is an error.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Report errors only, and disable the meter.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Also report device negotiation details.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list
List capture-capable PCM devices on standard output and exit.
.TP
.BR \-P ", " \-\-probe
Print the formats, channel counts, rates and buffer sizes the device supports,
then exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.SH KEEPING A TAKE
Once a take is closed and its size reported, audiaki offers it a folder and a
name:
.PP
.nf
.RS
audiaki: wrote ~/Takes/session\-001.wav: 42.10 s, 14.2 MiB, 0 xrun(s)
folder [~/Takes]:
name [session\-001.wav]: riff\-clean\-take.wav
audiaki: stored /home/you/Takes/riff\-clean\-take.wav
.RE
.fi
.PP
Enter accepts what is offered. Anything else moves the take there, creating the
folder if it has to. The questions go to standard error and are read from
standard input.
.PP
Nothing here can lose the recording. The file is complete and closed on disk
before the first question is asked; a name that is already taken is asked again
rather than overwritten, a move that fails reports where the take still is, and
Ctrl+C or Ctrl+D at either question keeps it where it was written.
.PP
The question is asked only when there is someone to answer it \(em both
standard input and standard error a terminal, and not
.BR \-\-quiet .
.B \-\-prompt
asks regardless, and
.B \-\-no\-prompt
never does. Neither is asked when
.B \-\-preroll
was interrupted while armed, because no file was created.
.SH METER
.PP
.nf
 00:12 [##################|          ]  \-8.4 dBFS  xruns:0
.fi
.PP
The bar spans \-60 dBFS to 0 dBFS. The
.B |
marker holds the highest peak seen so far.
.B CLIP
appears once a sample reaches full scale.
.B xruns
counts buffer overruns; a rising count means the machine could not keep up with
the device.
.PP
Under
.B \-\-spectrum
the bar is replaced by one column per frequency band, low frequencies on the
left, spaced logarithmically from 40 Hz to 12 kHz. The dBFS readout, peak
tracking and clipping warning are unchanged.
.PP
While
.B \-\-preroll
is waiting the line reads
.BR ARM ,
and the clock counts what is being held rather than what has been recorded: it
rises to the pre\-roll size and stops there, at the point a take would start
from. The peak hold, the clip flag and the xrun count all start again when the
take does, so the summary afterwards describes the take and not the wait.
.SH TUNER
.PP
.nf
 $ audiaki \-\-tune
 E2  [.................#.................]  in tune      82.4 Hz  \-18.3 dBFS
 A2  [...#.............|.................]  \-40 cents   107.5 Hz  \-22.0 dBFS
 \-\-  [.................|.................]  listening         \-\-  \-71.2 dBFS
.fi
.PP
The scale runs from half a semitone flat on the left to half a semitone sharp
on the right, with the note itself in the middle. The
.B #
marker is the pitch being played and the
.B |
is where it should be; past 50 cents the note name changes instead, so the
needle never points at the wrong note. A reading within 5 cents is called
.BR "in tune" ,
which is well inside what a string will drift on its own as it warms.
.PP
Pitch is found with the YIN difference function rather than by looking for the
loudest frequency. A plucked low string often has more energy in its second
harmonic than in its fundamental, and a tuner that followed the loudest
frequency would report the octave above. The tradeoff is that this finds one
pitch at a time: it is a tuner, not a chord detector.
.PP
Nothing quieter than about \-52 dBFS is treated as a note, so room noise does
not read as a pitch; a reading is held for a moment after the note decays past
that, so the display does not blink out between strums. Notes are named with
sharps, since a chromatic tuner has no key signature to choose otherwise.
.PP
When standard error is not a terminal there is no line to redraw in place, so
each note is reported once as it settles instead:
.PP
.nf
 $ audiaki \-\-tune 2> notes.log
 audiaki: E2  \-3 cents  82.2 Hz
 audiaki: A2  +1 cents  110.1 Hz
.fi
.SH TAKE REPORT
.PP
.nf
 $ audiaki \-\-info take01.wav
 file:        take01.wav
 format:      24 bit PCM
 channels:    2
 rate:        48000 Hz
 duration:    01:12.34  (3472320 frames)
 peak:        \-4.4 dBFS
 rms:         \-10.7 dBFS
 noise floor: \-68.2 dBFS
 clipped:     0 sample(s)
 channels:    ch 1: peak   \-4.4 dBFS  rms   \-8.7 dBFS  dc +0.00000
              ch 2: peak  \-10.5 dBFS  rms  \-14.7 dBFS  dc \-0.00029
 recorded:    2026\-08\-08 12:13:23
 device:      hw:CARD=Box,DEV=0
 software:    audiaki 1.0.0
 note:        second chorus, clean tone
.fi
.PP
The last four lines are what the file says about itself, and appear only when
it says it. See
.B TAKE METADATA
below.
.PP
.B peak
is the loudest single sample in the take and
.B rms
its average power, both in dBFS.
.B clipped
counts samples that reached full scale; anything above zero means the take is
distorted. Each channel is also measured on its own, so a clipped left channel
cannot hide behind a quiet right one, and a per-channel
.B dc
much past \(+-0.001 suggests the interface has a DC bias.
.PP
The
.B noise floor
is the tenth percentile of the RMS of 50 ms windows, which in practice
describes the room and the cable with nothing being played. Because it is a
percentile rather than a minimum, it survives a take that never falls silent; a
file that contains true digital silence reports \-99.0 dBFS, which is the truth
about it.
.PP
A file whose data chunk claims more frames than it holds \- a recording killed
before its header was patched \- is reported as truncated, and every figure
describes what is actually there.
.PP
Naming more than one file reports a row each instead, for triaging a session:
.PP
.nf
 $ audiaki \-\-info session\-*.wav
 FILE                DURATION     PEAK      RMS   CLIPPED
 session\-001.wav        41.20     \-4.4    \-10.7         0
 session\-002.wav      1:12.34     \-0.0     \-6.1      1820  CLIP
 session\-003.wav        38.05    \-14.2    \-22.8         0
.fi
.PP
A long name loses its middle rather than its end, because takes in a session
differ in the last few characters and a column of identical prefixes would not
say which take is which.
.SH TAKE METADATA
Every take audiaki records is stamped with what made it, when, and from what.
Two standard chunks go in ahead of the audio:
.TP
.B LIST/INFO
The tags taggers and most players already read: the software
.RB ( ISFT ),
the date
.RB ( ICRD ),
the capture device
.RB ( ISRC ,
which in RIFF/INFO means the source of the material rather than the music
industry's recording code) and the note from
.B \-\-note
.RB ( ICMT ).
.TP
.B bext
The Broadcast Wave extension, EBU Tech 3285, which every field recorder writes:
the origination date and time, a description, and a coding history line naming
the format and the device in the EBU R98 form
.IR "A=PCM,F=48000,W=24,M=stereo,T=audiaki 1.0.0; hw:CARD=Box,DEV=0" .
.IP
It also carries a
.BR "time reference" :
the number of samples between local midnight and the first frame of the take.
Two takes from one session line up on a timeline from that alone, without
either carrying timecode.
.PP
The time recorded is when the take started. With
.B \-\-preroll
that is the moment Enter was pressed, so the pre\-roll seconds are older than
the timestamp by exactly the pre\-roll \- the only reading of it that does not
need the pre\-roll length to make sense of.
.PP
None of it touches a sample of audio: the payload is the same interleaved PCM,
the whole stamp costs under a kilobyte, and the chunks sit between
.B fmt
and
.BR data ,
which is where the BWF specification requires bext and where they are already
on disk if a recording is interrupted.
.B \-\-no\-metadata
writes the plain 44 byte header instead.
.PP
.B \-\-info
reads the stamp back, and so does anything else that walks a chunk list:
.PP
.nf
 $ ffprobe \-show_format take01.wav
 TAG:comment=second chorus, clean tone
 TAG:date=2026\-08\-08
 TAG:coding_history=A=PCM,F=48000,W=24,M=stereo,T=audiaki 1.0.0; hw:CARD=Box,DEV=0
.fi
.PP
Only chunks ahead of the audio are read back. A file whose editor appended its
tags after the payload reads as having none.
.PP
.B audiaki\-gui
stamps its takes the same way, without a note: there is nowhere in the window
to type one.
.SH PLAYBACK
.B \-\-play
draws the meter described under
.B METER
with two differences: the clock shows a position within the file rather than a
length elapsed, and there is no xrun counter, nothing being written for frames
to be lost from.
.PP
.EX
 00:12 / 03:45 [##################|          ]  \-8.4 dBFS
.EE
.PP
.BR SIGINT " or " SIGTERM
stops at once. Reaching the end of the file, or the limit set by
.BR \-t ,
waits for the audio already queued to finish before the output is closed, so
the last of the take is not cut off.
.PP
Playback does not resample. Under PipeWire this is invisible, because the
server does; under ALSA the output must accept the file's own rate or audiaki
declines to play it, rather than play it back at the wrong pitch. See
.BR BACKENDS .
.PP
There is no seeking, no pausing and no playlist.
.B \-t
is the only way to hear less than the whole file.
.SH VISUALISER
Under
.B \-\-style bars
bands are analysed with a 2048 point Hann-windowed transform and spaced
logarithmically, so an octave occupies the same width wherever it falls. Each
band takes the strongest bin it covers, then rises quickly and falls slowly, so
a plucked note reads as an attack rather than a flicker. Bar colour runs from
cyan at the bass end to pink at the treble end, and the marker above each bar
holds its recent maximum.
.PP
.BR scope " and " waveform
draw the sample values rather than their spectrum, and share the same
background and palette.
.PP
.BR ffmpeg (1)
must be on
.B PATH
to render. It is not needed to build audiaki or to record with it. Frames are
handed over as raw RGBA and encoded with
.BR libx264 ,
with the audio re-encoded to AAC.
.PP
Rendering can be interrupted with
.BR SIGINT :
ffmpeg is stopped and the partial video is removed.
.SH DESKTOP APPLICATION
.B audiaki\-gui
is a multi\-track recorder and editor with the same capture core behind it: a
transport, a timeline, a live level meter with peak hold, and a real\-time
spectrum drawn as glowing bars in a panel that can be shut.
.PP
Recording goes onto the timeline at the cursor, on the selected track when it is
free from there on and on a new one otherwise, and the waveform is drawn as the
audio arrives rather than when the take stops. The take is a numbered WAV on
disk throughout, so an interrupted session is not a lost one.
.PP
The selection is a time range across one or more tracks, made by dragging.
.BR Cut ", " Copy ", " Paste ", " Delete ", " Silence ", " Trim ", " Split
and the two fades work on it, with sixty\-four steps of undo. None of them copies audio: a clip is
a window onto a block of samples that is never written to, so an edit costs the
same however long the take is.
.PP
.B Play
plays the selection, or from the cursor to the end, through the same mixer
.B Export
writes with \(em so what is heard and what is written cannot disagree. Mute,
solo, gain and pan apply to both.
.PP
The arrow keys drive the same edits without a pointer.
.BR \(<- " and " \(->
move the cursor by a few pixels' worth of time, so a nudge is the same gesture
at any zoom;
.B ctrl
steps clip edge to clip edge instead, and
.BR home " and " end
go to either end of the project. Holding
.B shift
moves the far end of the selection rather than the cursor, and
.BR \(ua " and " \(da
walk the track selection up and down the stack. The cursor is where the next
edit goes; the playhead is where the audio has got to, and is drawn separately
while anything is playing or recording.
.PP
A fade is a length on a clip rather than something written into the audio, so it
costs nothing and undoes like any other edit, and the waveform is drawn through
it. There are no crossfades: clips on a lane do not overlap, and a crossfade
needs two pieces of audio sounding at once.
.PP
What was done to a set of takes is a session, written to a
.B .aki
file by
.BR ctrl+S .
A session refers to its takes rather than containing them, so it is a few
kilobytes of readable text, and takes kept beside it are referred to relatively
so the folder can be moved as a unit.
.B audiaki \-\-render
mixes one down with no window involved. Closing the window with unsaved edits
writes them out rather than dropping them.
.PP
Files named on the command line are opened as tracks, and a
.B .aki
file among them is opened as the session.
.PP
The capture stream opens with the window and stays open whether or not a take is
being written, so the meter and the spectrum are live before recording starts \-
which is how an input level gets set in the first place.
.PP
A dropdown in the header selects the capture device, offering
.B default
alongside every capture PCM found. Switching rebuilds the stream and the
analyser around whatever the new device negotiates, and falls back to the
previous device if the new one will not open. It is disabled while a take is
open, because closing the capture stream mid\-take would truncate the recording.
.PP
The list follows the hardware. An interface plugged in appears in the dropdown a
moment later without a restart, and one unplugged leaves it. If the window comes
up with nothing to capture from \- or the device it was using is pulled out and
no other will open \- it waits on the "no device" screen with the dropdown still
live, and opens the next interface plugged in.
.TP
.B \-D, \-\-device NAME
Capture device the window opens on. It is preselected in the dropdown, and
appears there even when it is not one of the enumerated devices.
.TP
.B \-b, \-\-backend NAME
Audio system to talk to:
.BR auto ", " pipewire " or " alsa .
Chosen before the dropdown is first filled, so the list is whichever backend's.
See
.BR BACKENDS .
.TP
.B \-r, \-\-rate HZ
Sample rate.
.TP
.B \-c, \-\-channels N
Channel count.
.TP
.B \-o, \-\-take PREFIX
Take name prefix; takes are numbered from it. Defaults to
.BR take .
.TP
.B \-\-preroll SECS
Keep
.I SECS
of audio while idle, so pressing Record starts the take that far before the
press. The capture stream is already open, so nothing has to be armed: the
status line shows how much is held, and it is written to the front of the take
when one begins. Nothing is kept while recording or paused, and what was
prepended is not held a second time for the next take.
.TP
.B \-\-dir FOLDER
Keep takes in
.IR FOLDER ,
creating it if it is not there. The prefix above is written inside it.
Overrides
.B take_dir
from the configuration file; see
.BR FILES .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-dialog
Do not ask where a take should be kept when it stops; leave it where it was
recorded. The same as
.B prompt = no
in the configuration file.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-overdub
Do not play the project while recording over it. The default is to play along
to whatever is already on the timeline, which is what a second take is usually
for.
.TP
.B \-\-latency MS
Round\-trip latency to place an overdub by, 0 to 500 milliseconds. Playing along
to what is already there means hearing it late \(em the output holds a buffer,
then the input holds another \(em so the take is placed that much earlier than
Record was pressed, because that is when the sound was made.
.IP
Applied only when there is something to play along to. Recording from past the
end of the project, with overdubbing off, or with no output to play through,
nothing was heard late and the take starts exactly where it was asked for.
.IP
Estimated from the two queues when this is not given, which is a starting
point and not a measurement: the converters, the driver and the interface all
add delay nothing here can see. If overdubs land consistently late, measure the
round trip \(em play a click through the output into the input and see how far
off it lands \(em and set it. It belongs in
.B latency_ms
in the configuration file, being a property of the machine rather than of the
session. Zero turns the correction off.
.IP
What this cannot correct for is jitter: playback is fed from the drawing loop
and capture runs on its own thread, so the two start within a drawn frame of
each other rather than on the same sample.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-style NAME
Visualiser style to start on:
.BR bars ", " mirror ", " radial ", " scope ", " waterfall " or " tuner .
Defaults to
.BR bars .
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-video
Render an MP4 of the visualiser alongside each take, as if the
.B Video
control were already on.
.TP
.B \-\-video\-silent
Render that MP4 with no audio track, as if the
.B Audio
control were already off. The take's WAV is written as usual.
.TP
.B \-\-video\-size SPEC
Video size as
.IR WxH ,
or
.BR 480p ", " 720p ", " 1080p ", " 1440p " or " 2160p .
Defaults to 1280x720.
.TP
.B \-\-video\-fps N
Video frame rate, 1 to 240. Defaults to 60.
.TP
.B \-M, \-\-monitor
Start with playback monitoring already on.
.PP
Keys:
.B space
plays, or pauses and resumes a running take;
.BR R " or " ctrl+space
records from the cursor;
.B S
stops the take or playback, or cancels a video render;
.BR I " and " ctrl+E
import a WAV and export a mix;
.BR ctrl+X ", " ctrl+C " and " ctrl+V
cut, copy and paste,
.B del
deletes and
.B ctrl+Z
undoes;
.B B
shows and hides the visualiser panel;
.B M
toggles monitoring;
.B V
steps through the visualiser styles and
.BR 1 \- 6
pick one outright;
.B F
fits the selection or the project to the window,
.B F11
toggles fullscreen;
.B ?
puts the whole list over the window, and
.B Esc
takes it away again.
.PP
The same list is behind the
.B ?
in the header, and resting the pointer on any control says what it does and
which key does it. Disabled controls answer too, which is the point of them
having a tooltip at all: the transport greys out for a reason, and the reason
should be readable where the button is. The window title carries the transport
as well, so a window behind another one still says whether it is recording.
.PP
The styles all read the same analysis described under
.BR VISUALISER .
.BR bars ", " mirror " and " radial
draw the current spectrum as stems with glowing caps, from the floor, from the
centre line, and wrapped into a ring.
.B scope
draws raw samples instead, starting each sweep at a rising zero crossing so a
steady note stands still rather than scrolling, and showing true amplitude
rather than fitting the window.
.B waterfall
is the only one with a memory: it keeps around eight seconds of spectrogram
history, time running left to right and frequency bottom to top, so a hum or a
dropout is still on screen after it has happened.
.PP
.B tuner
is the odd one out. It shows a reading rather than a picture of the sound: the
note being played, a needle on a scale of half a semitone either side of it,
and the frequency against what that note should be, going green within 5 cents
and amber outside it. It runs the same detection as
.BR "audiaki \-\-tune" ,
described under
.BR TUNER ,
and only while it is the visible style \- the detection is not cheap, and
nothing else needs it. It sits with the visualiser styles because tuning up is
what happens immediately before pressing record.
.PP
Pausing stops appending to the file without closing it, so a take can be resumed
into the same WAV. Stopping patches the header and closes it.
.PP
Takes are always numbered from the prefix, so there is no
.B \-\-force
and pressing record cannot overwrite an earlier take.
.PP
With the
.B Video
control off a take is audio only. With it on, the same WAV is written and an
MP4 of the visualiser is rendered beside it, carrying the take's own audio.
This needs
.BR ffmpeg (1)
on
.BR PATH ;
without it the WAV is still written and the window reports why the video was
not.
.PP
The
.B Audio
control beside it decides whether that MP4 has an audio track at all. It is on
by default; with it off, or with
.BR \-\-video\-silent ,
the video is encoded with no audio stream, for a clip going into an edit that
has the sound already or somewhere it should not play. The take is still read
either way - it is what the picture is drawn from - and its WAV is written
whichever way the control is set. Both controls are settable only between
takes: the render is a single pass over the finished take, so there is no half
of it to change your mind about, and
.B Audio
greys out with
.B Video
off.
.PP
The video is rendered from the finished take when recording stops, rather than
captured live from the screen. Recording is the job that cannot miss a
deadline, and reading back the framebuffer sixty times a second while a capture
stream is open is how takes acquire xruns. Rendering afterwards costs the wait
but is frame accurate, is independent of the window's size and refresh rate,
and cannot drop a frame. While it runs,
.B Stop
becomes
.BR Cancel ;
cancelling removes the partial video and keeps the take.
.PP
The video file appears only when it is complete. An MP4 cannot be played until
its index is written, which happens as the encoder finishes, so the frames are
written to a hidden
.I .NAME.partial.mp4
and the real name is created by
.BR rename (2)
at the end. A cancelled, failed or interrupted render therefore leaves the take
and no video, rather than a file that looks like a video and will not open.
.PP
For rendering without a display - on a headless machine, or from a script - use
.B \-\-visualize
instead, which reads a WAV that already exists.
.PP
Monitoring opens a second stream on the default output and plays the input back
through it. It is off unless
.B \-M
is given, because monitoring a microphone through speakers howls. audiaki does
not resample, so an output that will not accept the capture rate is declined
rather than played back at the wrong pitch. When the output cannot keep up the
excess frames are dropped rather than queued, so the monitor stays in step with
the input instead of drifting further behind it.
.PP
.B audiaki\-gui
is only built when the vendored raylib submodule is initialised and the OpenGL
and X11 development headers are installed. Neither is needed for
.BR audiaki .
.SH BACKENDS
.B audiaki
reaches the hardware through one of two audio systems.
.TP
.B pipewire
Asks the sound server that owns the card. It shares the interface with anything
else using it, names devices as the rest of the desktop does, is told at once
when hardware appears or goes, and provides any format or rate asked for by
conversion. A PipeWire sink is offered as a capture device too: recording one
captures what is being played to it, which is how another application's output
is recorded. Monitoring works at any rate, because the server resamples.
Requires a running daemon, and a build with the PipeWire headers present.
.TP
.B alsa
Opens the card directly through libasound, with nothing in between. One program
at a time holds a device, so a busy card fails rather than being shared, and the
format is whatever the hardware genuinely offers \(em which is what makes
.B \-\-probe
meaningful here and advisory under PipeWire. Needs no daemon.
.PP
Which one is in use shows in the
.B backend
field of
.BR "\-\-list \-\-json" " and " "\-\-probe \-\-json" ,
and in
.B \-v
output. Run
.B make help
to see which backends a build has.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B AUDIAKI_DEVICE
Default capture device, used when
.B \-D
is not given. Ignored by
.BR \-\-play ,
which opens an output rather than an input.
.TP
.B AUDIAKI_BACKEND
Default backend
.RB ( auto ", " pipewire " or " alsa ),
used when
.B \-\-backend
is not given. An unrecognised value warns and is ignored rather than failing, so
a typo in an exported variable cannot make every invocation \(em including
.BR \-\-help " \(em" " fail."
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/audiaki/config
Settings that outlive an invocation. Overridden by
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/audiaki/config
and by
.IR $AUDIAKI_CONFIG ,
which names a file rather than a directory. Everything in it is a default the
command line can still override, and it is not an error for it to be absent.
.IP
One
.I key = value
per line;
.B #
and
.B ;
start a comment, and a line that cannot be understood is reported and skipped.
Three settings are recognised:
.RS
.TP
.B take_dir
Where a take named without a folder is written, as
.BR \-\-dir .
A leading
.B ~
is expanded. Empty means the working directory.
.TP
.B prompt
Whether to ask where a finished take should be kept:
.B auto
(only when there is a terminal to ask at),
.B yes
or
.BR no .
Defaults to
.BR auto .
The desktop application reads the same setting, where
.B no
means its save dialog does not open.
.TP
.B latency_ms
Round-trip latency the desktop application places an overdub by, in
milliseconds, 0 to 500. Playing along to what is already recorded means hearing
it late, so the take is placed that much earlier than the button was pressed.
Left out, it is estimated from the capture and playback buffers, which is a
starting point rather than a measurement \- see
.BR audiaki\-gui (1).
.TP
.B gain
Gain added to the recording itself, as a multiplier from 0.0 to 16.0. The same
setting as
.BR \-\-gain ,
and worth keeping here rather than typing when it corrects a property of the
interface rather than of the session. The desktop application reads it too, as
the starting position of its input gain slider. Left out, the take is the
samples the device delivered.
.RE
.IP
For example:
.IP
.nf
.RS
take_dir = ~/Takes
prompt = auto
latency_ms = 14
gain = 2.0
.RE
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Recording completed, or the requested information was printed.
.TP
.B 1
The device could not be opened or configured, the file could not be written, or
a render failed.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was malformed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Record until interrupted:
.PP
.nf
.RS
audiaki take01.wav
.RE
.fi
.PP
Tune up first, then record:
.PP
.nf
.RS
audiaki \-\-tune
audiaki \-\-take session
.RE
.fi
.PP
Keep a session's takes together, and be asked about each one as it finishes:
.PP
.nf
.RS
audiaki \-\-dir ~/Takes/2026\-08\-09 \-\-take session
.RE
.fi
.PP
Tune to an ensemble at A = 432 Hz:
.PP
.nf
.RS
audiaki \-\-tune \-\-a4 432
.RE
.fi
.PP
Record 90 seconds from a specific device at 48 kHz:
.PP
.nf
.RS
audiaki \-D plughw:CARD=Box,DEV=0 \-r 48000 \-t 1:30 take02.wav
.RE
.fi
.PP
Hear the take through headphones on the interface while it is recorded:
.PP
.nf
.RS
audiaki \-D plughw:CARD=Box,DEV=0 \-M \-\-monitor\-device plughw:CARD=Box,DEV=0 take03.wav
.RE
.fi
.PP
Play along to a metronome at 120 BPM, four to the bar, hearing yourself:
.PP
.nf
.RS
audiaki \-M \-\-click 120 take04.wav
.RE
.fi
.PP
Count yourself in over eight seconds of pre\-roll, three to the bar:
.PP
.nf
.RS
audiaki \-\-preroll 8 \-\-click 140 \-\-click\-beats 3 \-\-take waltz
.RE
.fi
.PP
Watch the spectrum while playing, then render the take to 1080p:
.PP
.nf
.RS
audiaki \-\-spectrum take01.wav
audiaki \-\-visualize take01.wav \-\-size 1080p
.RE
.fi
.PP
Track a part over several attempts, then check how they came out:
.PP
.nf
.RS
audiaki \-\-take session \-\-note "clean tone"
audiaki \-\-take session \-\-note "with the pedal"
audiaki \-\-info session\-*.wav
.RE
.fi
.PP
Find the takes that clipped, out of a session's worth:
.PP
.nf
.RS
audiaki \-\-info session\-*.wav \-\-json | jq \-r '.[] | select(.clipped_samples > 0).file'
.RE
.fi
.PP
Listen back to it:
.PP
.nf
.RS
audiaki \-\-play session\-002.wav
.RE
.fi
.PP
Render the whole take as a waveform with a playhead:
.PP
.nf
.RS
audiaki \-\-visualize session\-002.wav \-\-style waveform
.RE
.fi
.PP
Find every take that clipped:
.PP
.nf
.RS
for f in *.wav; do
  n=$(audiaki \-\-info "$f" \-\-json | jq .clipped_samples)
  [ "$n" \-gt 0 ] && echo "$f: $n clipped"
done
.RE
.fi
.SH NOTES
Little-endian hosts only. Output is plain 44-byte-header PCM WAV, so a
recording stops at the 4 GB RIFF size limit.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR arecord (1),
.BR alsamixer (1),
.BR ffmpeg (1)
.SH BUGS
Report issues at https://github.com/HilthonTT/audiaki/issues
